\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression results (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels constructed from the 15 judges in Table~\ref{tab:models}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Bench.} & \textbf{Attack} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Dom.} \\
\midrule
MMLU & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
MMLU & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Position bias  & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
MMLU & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
\addlinespace
ARC  & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $-^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
ARC  & Position bias  & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficients for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across 12 conditions (455 panels, log-log OLS). $+^{*}$/$-^{*}$: FDR-significant positive/negative (Benjamini--Hochberg, $q < 0.05$); n.s.: not significant. Dom.\ column: $\eta$ = $\eta_{\max}$ sole dominant, $K$ = $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sole dominant, Co = co-dominant \citep{budescu1993dominance}.}
\label{tab:dominance}
\end{table}

The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}$ is the stronger predictor under OLS, reaching FDR significance in 11 of 12 conditions. The sole exception is ARC $\times$ position bias ($p = 0.909$). This near-universal significance is consistent with the prediction of Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}: the most vulnerable panel member is the primary channel through which adaptive attacks succeed.

Panel diversity $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is FDR-significant positive in 7 of 12 conditions, not significant in 4 (MMLU $\times$ \{score manipulation, position bias, authority bias\} and ARC $\times$ verbosity), and FDR-significant negative in 1 (ARC $\times$ sycophancy, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$, FDR $p = 0.013$). Under OLS, the 7-to-1 ratio of positive to negative significant effects suggests that diversity is predominantly associated with increased vulnerability under adaptive attack, counter to the non-adaptive prediction of the SDPI bound (\S\ref{sec:threat_model}).

These OLS estimates, however, treat the 455 panels as independent observations. They are not: each of the 15 judges appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, so panel-level residuals are correlated. Corrected standard errors are approximately $2.5\times$ larger than OLS estimates. Section~\ref{sec:robustness} tests the robustness of both predictors under three correction methods.

Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} under OLS yields 4 $\eta_{\max}$-dominant conditions, 7 co-dominant, and 1 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant (ARC $\times$ position bias, where $\eta_{\max}$ is not significant). Under OLS assumptions, neither predictor uniformly dominates.

The $r = 0.70$ correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ raises a confound concern: the positive diversity coefficient might merely proxy for weakest-link vulnerability. Under OLS, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains independent predictive power in 8 of 12 conditions (7 positive, 1 negative), and the sign reversal in ARC $\times$ sycophancy disproves pure confounding. Under corrected inference (\S\ref{sec:robustness}), however, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent predictive power is substantially weaker, with significance dropping to 0--1 of 12 conditions under the most reliable correction methods.

% TODO: Figure 1 here — scatter plot of η_max vs ASR across 455 panels, colored by K_eff

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness}

The 455 panels comprise all $\binom{15}{3}$ triples of 15 judges. Each judge therefore appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, and each pair of judges co-occurs in 13. This shared structure induces correlated residuals in the OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}), causing OLS to underestimate standard errors by a factor of approximately $2.5$. Table~\ref{tab:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections that account for this non-independence.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} & $\boldsymbol{\eta}$\textbf{:}$\boldsymbol{K}$ \\
\midrule
OLS                   & 11/12 & 8/12 & 7+/1$-$ & 1.4:1 \\
Clustered SE (CR1)    & 8/12  & 0/12 & ---     & ---   \\
Mixed effects         & 7/12  & 3/12 & 3+/0$-$ & 2.3:1 \\
Wild cluster bootstrap & 5/12  & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ & 5:1   \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Robustness of regression results to non-independence correction. Clustered SE uses 3-position max-SE with CR1 correction (13 clusters per position). Mixed effects uses 3-position max-$p$ with random judge intercepts. Wild cluster bootstrap uses $B{=}9999$ resamples, Rademacher weights, and max-$p$ across 3 panel positions. $\eta$:$K$ ratio = $\eta_{\max}$ significant conditions / $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant conditions.}
\label{tab:robustness}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ remains a robust predictor across all correction methods: 8/12 under clustered standard errors, 7/12 under mixed effects, and 5/12 under the most conservative wild cluster bootstrap.\footnote{The bootstrap's max-across-positions bound is driven by position~0's extreme cluster imbalance (one cluster contains 91 panels, another only 1); per-position estimates (9/12 each for positions 1 and 2) align with the clustered SE result of 8/12.} The bootstrap attrition concentrates in ARC conditions (1/6 survive) rather than MMLU (4/6 survive). This benchmark asymmetry likely reflects the greater scoring variance in MMLU's 4-choice format compared to ARC's 2-choice format, which provides more statistical power for detecting $\eta_{\max}$ effects.

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses under correction. OLS yields 8/12 FDR-significant conditions (7 positive, 1 negative), but clustered standard errors reduce this to 0/12 and wild cluster bootstrap to 1/12. Point estimates remain predominantly positive, but confidence intervals contain zero in 11 of 12 conditions under bootstrap. The $\eta_{\max}$:$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance ratio shifts from 1.4:1 under OLS to 5:1 under bootstrap: $\eta_{\max}$ robustly predicts panel vulnerability, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent contribution is not statistically distinguishable from zero after correcting for shared judge structure.

The practical effect size reinforces this conclusion. A change in $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across the interquartile range is associated with $\Delta\mathrm{ASR} = 1.3$ percentage points. Even under OLS, where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ appears FDR-significant in 8/12 conditions, the diversity effect is small in absolute terms.

The ARC $\times$ sycophancy reversal (the sole negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient under OLS, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$, FDR $p = 0.013$) is also downgraded under correction: clustered SE $p = 0.441$, mixed effects $p = 0.136$, bootstrap $p \approx 0.06$. This condition is no longer FDR-significant under any correction method and is treated as a directional observation only.

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

Panel composition is far more predictive of vulnerability under random faults than under adaptive attack. We simulate non-adversarial evaluation noise by randomly flipping judge votes at rates matching observed per-judge error distributions, then re-run the corrected regressions. Under random faults, both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ are significant positive predictors of panel error in both benchmark conditions, surviving all correction methods ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: clustered SE $p = 3.7 \times 10^{-4}$ and $0.009$; bootstrap $p = 0.004$ and $0.019$; $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = +0.215$ and $+0.379$). The regression $R^2$ ranges from 0.73 to 0.81, indicating that panel composition is highly predictable under non-adaptive faults. Under adaptive attack, this predictability breaks down: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s apparent significance is an artifact of panel overlap (0--1/12 after correction), while adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance beyond what panel structure can explain. The contrast is one of genuineness: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association with vulnerability is real under random faults but inflated under adaptive attack.

% TODO: Figure 2 here — GAM partial effects of K_eff, split by targeted vs shared-bias attacks (6:6 pattern)

\subsection{Attack-Type Taxonomy}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM analysis of the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect reveals structure masked by the significance collapse documented in \S\ref{sec:robustness}. The 12 conditions split cleanly into two regimes by attack mechanism. \textit{Targeted attacks} exploit judge-specific parsing or format vulnerabilities (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias): the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect is positive in all 6 conditions (2 benchmarks $\times$ 3 attacks). Diverse panels expose more distinct weaknesses for the adversary to exploit. \textit{Shared-bias attacks} exploit cognitive biases common across architectures (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias): the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect is negative in all 6 conditions. Diverse panels dilute shared susceptibility rather than exposing new attack surface.

This 6:6 split is obscured by linear OLS, which averages over the two regimes and reports a net 7+/1$-$/4NS pattern. The taxonomy provides mechanistic explanatory power: the \emph{direction} of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect is predictable from attack type, even when its \emph{magnitude} is not statistically significant after non-independence correction. GAM partial effects require bootstrapped confidence intervals for formal significance testing (pending); the structural 6:6 pattern is the primary contribution of this analysis.

% TODO: Figure 3 here — aggregation gradient: R² by method (threshold → majority → weighted → bayesian)

\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

The vulnerability driver effect varies substantially across aggregation methods. Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for each method across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.890 \\
Majority        & 11/12 & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.298 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 9/12  & 9+/0$-$   & 0.284 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 7/12  & 1+/6$-$   & 0.104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison (15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}

Four patterns emerge. First, the aggregation methods form a clear vulnerability gradient: threshold voting ($R^2{=}0.890$) makes panel composition almost perfectly predictive of attack success, while Bayesian calibration ($R^2{=}0.104$) compresses the ASR distribution so that compositional factors explain little variance. Second, Bayesian calibration reverses the diversity channel: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance is comparable (8/12 $\to$ 7/12), but the coefficient direction reverses from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/6$-$), meaning diversity shifts from being associated with higher vulnerability to being associated with lower vulnerability under proper reliability calibration. Bayesian also partially weakens $\eta_{\max}$ (11/12 $\to$ 9/12). Third, threshold voting amplifies both channels: $\eta_{\max}$ reaches 12/12 and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reaches 11/12, because unanimity requirements make panels maximally sensitive to both individual weakness and structural diversity. Fourth, weighted voting weakens the $\eta_{\max}$ channel (11/12 $\to$ 9/12) but the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ channel remains uniformly positive (9/12, all positive), indicating that simple accuracy weighting does not mitigate diversity-as-vulnerability. These aggregation results use OLS and are structurally independent of the non-independence concern in \S\ref{sec:robustness}: changing the aggregation rule alters the dependent variable, not the panel overlap structure.

These results yield a prescriptive gradient. Bayesian calibration is the only aggregation method that reverses the diversity channel (in 6 of 7 significant conditions, the diversity coefficient is negative rather than positive) and partially weakens the weakest-link channel, but it does not eliminate either. A two-pronged defense is needed: robustness-first panel selection to address $\eta_{\max}$, combined with Bayesian aggregation to convert $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ from a vulnerability into a defense. Neither intervention alone addresses both drivers.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API; six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with AWQ INT4 quantization on an NVIDIA RTX~6000 (96\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). All panels consist of $K{=}3$ judges with majority-vote threshold $t{=}2$. Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ unique panels, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lllccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Deploy} & \textbf{MMLU} & \textbf{ARC} \\
\midrule
gpt-5.5              & OpenAI    & frontier    & API   & .957 & .983 \\
gemini-3.1-pro-preview & Google  & frontier    & API   & .955 & .987 \\
claude-opus-4-6       & Anthropic & frontier   & API   & .890 & .987 \\
\addlinespace
gemini-3.5-flash      & Google    & mid-tier   & API   & .968 & 1.00$^\dagger$ \\
gpt-4.1               & OpenAI    & mid-tier   & API   & .837 & .967 \\
claude-sonnet-4-6     & Anthropic & mid-tier   & API   & .810 & .953 \\
\addlinespace
gpt-4.1-mini          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .800 & .963 \\
gpt-4.1-nano          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .633 & .853 \\
claude-3-haiku        & Anthropic & lightweight & API  & .767 & .450 \\
mistral-large (123B)  & Mistral   & lightweight & local & .537 & .533 \\
llama3.1-70b          & Llama     & lightweight & local & .523 & .520 \\
llama3.1-8b           & Llama     & lightweight & local & .543 & .320 \\
qwen2.5-72b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .333 & .410 \\
qwen2.5-32b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .373 & .450 \\
qwen2.5-14b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .253 & .240 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Judge models used in the study (clean accuracy on 300-item samples). API models accessed via OpenRouter; local models served with vLLM + AWQ INT4 quantization. $^\dagger$\,gemini-3.5-flash achieves perfect ARC accuracy but with 26.3\% format error rate.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each benchmark. MMLU probes factual recall and broad domain knowledge; ARC requires multi-step scientific reasoning. All API calls use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs; local models are served with the same temperature setting. Attacked variants are generated by programmatically applying each manipulation to the candidate text, with a fixed random seed for reproducibility.

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types that target different judge vulnerabilities: (1)~\textbf{prompt injection}, adversarial instructions embedded in candidate text that override the evaluation prompt \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}; (2)~\textbf{sycophancy}, responses engineered to agree with or flatter the judge \citep{wang2023fair}; (3)~\textbf{score manipulation}, explicit numerical cues (e.g., ``this answer deserves a 10/10'') injected into the candidate to anchor scoring; (4)~\textbf{verbosity bias}, padding correct answers with excessive irrelevant detail to exploit length preferences \citep{zheng2023judging}; (5)~\textbf{position bias}, reordering candidate answers to place the target in the position judges systematically favor \citep{wang2023fair}; and (6)~\textbf{authority bias}, prefacing candidate text with fabricated credentials or citations to inflate perceived credibility. The combination of 2 benchmarks and 6 attacks produces 12 experimental conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels.

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Per-judge vulnerability under attack $a$ is the fraction of items where judge $j$ is fooled: $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$, estimated empirically over the item set. The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the most susceptible member of each panel. The panel attack success rate $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ is the fraction of items where at least $t$ judges are simultaneously fooled. Panel diversity is quantified by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), computed from pairwise mutual information between judges' three-category winner labels (model~A wins, model~B wins, tie) on clean (unattacked) data. MI is estimated via the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow bias correction. In practice, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is computed through the Gaussian copula correlation matrix: under the Gaussian assumption, pairwise MI and copula $\rho$ are related by $\mathrm{MI} = -\tfrac{1}{2}\log(1-\rho^2)$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}, and we substitute $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}) = 1 - \rho^2$ directly into Equation~\ref{eq:keff}. Together, $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ serve as the two predictors in the regression framework below: $\eta_{\max}$ indexes the weakest-link channel and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ indexes the diversity channel.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond the default majority vote, we evaluate three alternative aggregation rules to test whether the diversity-vulnerability association persists under different decision mechanisms. The aggregation comparison uses the same log-log regression specification as the primary analysis (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}).

\paragraph{Majority Vote.} Accept if $\sum_{j=1}^K \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] \geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$. This is the standard baseline aggregation assumed throughout the main analysis.

\paragraph{Accuracy-Weighted Vote.} Accept if $\sum_{j=1}^K w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$, where $w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$ on held-out clean data, normalized so that $\sum_j w_j = 1$. Judges with higher clean-data accuracy receive proportionally greater influence.

\paragraph{Threshold Vote (Unanimity).} Accept only if all $K$ judges approve ($t = K$). A single dissenting judge suffices to reject, making this the most conservative rule.

\paragraph{Bayesian Calibration.} Compute $P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$, where $P(v_j \mid \cdot)$ is derived from judge $j$'s confusion matrix on clean data and $\mathbf{v}$ is the observed vote vector. Accept if the posterior exceeds 0.5. This rule uses each judge's calibrated reliability rather than treating all votes equally.

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To disentangle the contributions of panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values. Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate per predictor across the 12 conditions.

To assess relative predictor importance beyond regression coefficients, we perform dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance}. This method decomposes $R^2$ by averaging each predictor's incremental $R^2$ contribution across all possible subsets of predictors. A predictor is \emph{dominant} if its average contribution exceeds that of the competing predictor in every subset ordering. With two predictors, dominance reduces to comparing each predictor's solo $R^2$ with its incremental $R^2$ conditional on the other.

To test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect direction varies by attack type, we fit generalized additive models (GAMs) with smooth terms for both predictors, allowing nonlinear partial effects. The GAM partial effect of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is then examined separately for targeted attacks (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) and shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias).

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels are constructed combinatorially from 15 shared judges: each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, inducing correlated residuals across panel-level observations. Standard OLS assumes independent errors, potentially inflating significance. We apply three corrections.

\paragraph{Clustered Standard Errors.} Because each panel of $K{=}3$ judges does not map to a single cluster, we perform three separate one-way clusterings---one for each judge position in the ordered panel tuple---using CR1 bias-corrected sandwich estimators (13 clusters per position). The reported standard error for each coefficient is the maximum across the three clusterings, yielding the most conservative inference.

\paragraph{Mixed Effects.} For each of the three judge positions, we fit a linear mixed-effects model with a random intercept for the judge occupying that position, estimating $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ as fixed effects. The reported $p$-value for each coefficient is the maximum across the three models.

\paragraph{Wild Cluster Bootstrap.} We perform $B{=}9{,}999$ bootstrap resamples using Rademacher weights, clustering by each of the three panel positions in turn and taking the maximum $p$-value across positions (the most conservative aggregation). This approach avoids distributional assumptions on the cluster structure.

\subsection{Sensitivity Analysis}

Judges with clean-data accuracy below 0.5 perform at or below chance and could drive regression results through extreme outlier behavior rather than meaningful vulnerability patterns. We exclude such judges from panel construction and re-run the log-log regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) to verify that the $\eta_{\max}$ effect remains stable after removing these potential confounds.

